'''Cross'''

* The red and gold, derived directly from the flag of Occitania, symbolize the Occitan history and heritage of Catharism; Occitania being the homeland of the spiritual movement, beginning in the Middle Ages.

* The red represents in itself the fires of the {{W|Albigensian Crusade}} and the blood shed by both Cathar and sympathizer/defender alike.

* The gold in itself represents the Light which descends from Heaven and the yellow cross which Cathars were required to wear if they abandoned the faith.

* The wavy lines represent flames and light rays.  

'''Dove'''

* The Cathar dove, also known as the Minerve dove, represents the Cathar faithful who are called to be ‘as gentle as doves’ and the Holy Spirit.

* The white of the dove here represents the purity of the Cathar way.

* The black outline of the dove represents both the darkness out of which the Light shines and the dark robes worn by Mediaeval Cathar Perfecti.

* The black and white taken as a whole represent duality, which is central to the Cathar faith.
