{{Culture of Balisca}}

'''Catharism''' (from the {{lang-grc|καθαροί|katharoi }}, {{Literal translation|the pure ones}}), is a {{W|Christianity|Christian}} [[w:Dualistic cosmology|dualist]] or [[w:Gnosticism|Gnostic]] movement originating in {{W|Southern Europe}}, particularly in {{W|Occitania}} and {{W|Southern France}}. Followers, described as '''Cathars''', and often referring to themselves as '''Good Christians''', were subjected to a prolonged period of {{W|religious persecution}} by the {{W|Catholic Church}}, which to this day does not recognize their unorthodox Christianity. Catharism arrived in Western Europe in the {{W|Languedoc}} region of Occitania in the 11th century. The adherents were sometimes referred to as '''Albigensians''', after the city {{W|Albi}} in what is now southern France where the movement first took hold. The belief is determined to have originated in the {{W|Byzantine Empire}}. Catharism was initially taught by [[w:Asceticism|ascetic]] leaders who set few guidelines and so some Catharist practices and beliefs varied by region and over time. The Catholic Church has continuously denounced all of its practices since it's birth, including it's acceptance of {{W|gender equality}} and tolerance of {{W|homosexuality}}, along with the ''{{W|consolamentum}}'' ritual by which Cathar individuals are baptized and raised to the status of "[[w:Cathar Perfect|Perfect]]". Cathars today are almost entirely residing within [[Balisca]], although small communities have survived throughout the former {{W|Spanish colonial empire}}.

Catharism was greatly influenced by the [[w:Bogomilism|Bogomils]] of the {{W|First Bulgarian Empire}}, and is thought to also have roots in the [[w:Paulicianism|Paulician]] movement in {{W|Armenia}} and eastern {{W|Byzantine}} {{W|Anatolia}} through Paulicians resettled in {{W|Thrace}} ([[w:Plovdiv|Philippopolis]]). Though the term ''Cathar'' has been used for centuries to identify the movement, whether it identified itself with the name is debated. In present day Cathar texts, the terms ''Good One'' ({{lang|ca|bona}}) or ''Good Christians'' ({{lang|ca|bon cristià}}) are the common terms of {{W|self-identification}}.

The idea of two gods or deistic principles, one good and the other evil, is the main point of criticism asserted by the Catholic Church against Cathar beliefs. The Catholic Church asserts this was antithetical to {{W|monotheism}}, a fundamental principle that there is only one "God", who created all things visible and invisible, which is known as the {{W|Nicene Creed}}. Cathars believed that the "good {{W|God}}" was the god of the {{W|New Testament}}, creator of the spiritual realm, whereas the "evil God" was the god of the {{W|Old Testament}}, creator of the physical world whom many Cathars identified as {{W|Satan}}. Cathars believe human [[w:Soul|spirit]]s are the {{W|sexless}} spirits of {{W|angel}}s trapped in the material realm of the "evil God", destined to be [[w:reincarnation|reincarnated]] until they achieved salvation through the {{W|consolamentum}}, a form of baptism performed when death is imminent, when they would return to the good God.

From the beginning of his reign, {{W|Pope Innocent III}} attempted to end Catharism by sending missionaries and by persuading the local authorities to act against them. In 1208, {{W|Pierre de Castelnau}}, Innocent's {{W|papal legate}}, was murdered while returning to {{W|Rome}} after excommunicating [[w:Raymond VI, Count of Toulouse|Count Raymond VI of Toulouse]], who, in his view, was too lenient with the Cathars. Pope Innocent III then abandoned the option of sending Catholic [[w:missionary|missionaries]] and {{W|jurist}}s, declared Pierre de Castelnau a [[w:Christian martyrs|martyr]] and launched the [[Albigensian Crusade]] in 1209. The Crusade ended in 1229 with the withdrawal of the Crusaders following a string of victories by the armies of the {{W|Crown of Aragon}}. Following the incorporation of the Catharist into the Aragonese society, Catharism settlers spread throughout the {{W|Iberian Peninsula}} and went on to pioneer many of the Iberian expeditions to the {{W|New World}}.

